In quantum mechanics observables are represented not by functions on state-space points but by linear operators. In a finite-dimensional state space, those operators are matrices. A matrix $M$ maps one vector to another: $M\ket a=\ket{a'}.$ For two components, $\begin{pmatrix}
    m_{11}&m_{12}\\
    m_{21}&m_{22}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}a_1\\a_2\end{pmatrix}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    m_{11}a_1+m_{12}a_2\\
    m_{21}a_1+m_{22}a_2
  \end{pmatrix}.$

